<SubSection title="Conduction" id="Conduction">
<p> Conductive heat transfer is the transport of thermal energy through a medium due to temperature gradients within the material. Energy is transferred down the temperature gradient. The method of heat transfer is called diffusion. Diffusion is the net transfer of energy due to random molecular motion. 
</p>
<p> Fourier’s equation is used to describe conductive heat transfer. You can calculate heat flow and heat flux using Fourier's equation. 
</p>
<p> Heat Flow: </p>
<DisplayEquation equation="\\frac{q_x}{A} = -k \\frac{dT}{dx}" title="" />
<p> Heat Flux: </p>
<DisplayEquation equation="q^{''}_x = -k \\frac{dT}{dx}" title="" />
<p> Lets go over the variables present in these equations: </p>
<Itemize>

<Item><InlineEquation equation="q_x (W)" /> = rate of heat flow in the x direction </Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="A (m^2)" /> = area perpendicular to the direction of heat flow</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="q^{''}_x (\\frac{W}{m^2})" /> = rate of heat flow per unit area</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="T (C or K)" /> = temperature of the medium at x</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="k (\\frac{W}{mC} or \\frac{W}{mK})" /> = thermal conductivity of the medium</Item>

</Itemize>
</SubSection>

<SubSubSection title="Steady State" id="Steady State">
<p> In steady state heat conduction, temperature does not change with time. However, temperature can change with positon and this is what drives the transfer of heat. There are three main examples of steady state heat conduction: </p>
<Itemize>

<Item>In a slab</Item>
<Item>In a cylinder</Item>
<Item>With heat generation (Q)</Item>

</Itemize>

</SubSubSection>

<SubSubSection title="Unsteady State" id="Unsteady State">
<p> In unsteady state heat conduction, temperature changes over time. There are three main examples of unsteady state heat conduction: </p>
<Itemize>

<Item>With no spatial variation</Item>
<Item>With semi-infinite geometry</Item>
<Item>With finite geometry</Item>

</Itemize>
</SubSubSection>

<SubSection title="Convection" id="Convection">
<p> Convective heat transfer is the transport of thermal energy due to bulk motion of a fluid over a surface. </p>
<p> The equation that describes convection is </p>
<DisplayEquation equation="q_{1-2} = hA(T_1 - T_2)" title="" />
<p> Lets go over the variables present in this equation: </p>
<Itemize>

<Item><InlineEquation equation="q_{1-2} (W)" /> = rate of heat flow from 1 to 2 </Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="A (m^2)" /> = area normal to the direction of heat flow</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="T_1 - T_2 (C)" /> = temperature difference between the surface and fluid</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="h (\\frac{W}{m^2C})" /> = convective heat transfer coefficient (this coefficient always includes the effect of conduction in the fluid)</Item>

</Itemize>
</SubSection>

